<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Dientzenhofer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1663">1663</a>–1726</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Dientzenhofer was a German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> architect from a Bavarian family of builders. He designed Fulda Cathedral, built from 1704 to 1712, as well as the abbey church of Banz and the palace of Pommersfelden for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schonborn-family">Schönborn family</a>. His work brought Italian Baroque forms to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-dientzenhofer.jpg" alt="Johann Dientzenhofer" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steffen Schmitz (more photos) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fulda,_Rittergasse_4,_2019-10_CN-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
